=============================================================================
WHY THIS EXISTS, AND IT IS NOT TIDINESS
=============================================================================

The live gate was a SENTENCE in the plan documents before it was a script:

    "a live map still loads at 473 entities / 61 classes; make check green"

Everybody honoured it, and everybody honoured it the same way -- launch,
`tools/debug-api ents "*"`, read the two numbers, stop. ONE MAP LOAD.

A crash lived on the integration branch for weeks behind exactly that shape.
ffd3e097 left CWorld with `Release() {}` after un-deriving it from CBaseEntity,
so the entity purge -- which destroys everything outside the SDK by calling
Release() -- stopped freeing the world. Nothing about the FIRST map is wrong
when that happens: dm_lockdown loads, reads 473 / 61, paints its decals and
plays. The SECOND load is where the previous world is still standing, the new
one is refused, and the first trace of level init faults inside the engine.

Every lane's gate passed over it. Not one of them was wrong about what it
measured; they were all measuring a game that had loaded one map.

So plan_entity.md step 0d asks for the whole checklist TWICE IN ONE PROCESS,
and that is why --passes defaults to 2 and why --passes 1 prints that it is not
the gate. A checklist that passes once proves much less than one that passes
after the world has already been torn down and rebuilt underneath it.

=============================================================================
THE SIXTEEN STEPS (plan_entity.md §5, in the order they are listed there)
=============================================================================

    join . move . jump . shoot and hit . take damage . pick up health and ammo .
    enter and leave a trigger . open a door . ride a lift . push a physics prop .
    die and ragdoll . respawn . second client joins and both see each other .
    hot reload with the world intact . map reload . clean shutdown with every
    entity destroyed.

They RUN in a different order than they are listed, because several of them set
each other up -- the damage step is what makes a health pickup takeable, the
ammo step is what puts rounds in the gun the shooting step fires -- and the
three terminal ones (hot reload, map reload, shutdown) have to be last or there
is nothing left to test. The listed order is kept as the REPORTING order and
`--self-test` asserts the registry still carries all sixteen with §5's names.

=============================================================================
ASSERTED THROUGH tools/debug-api, NOT GREPPED OUT OF A LOG
=============================================================================

§5 is explicit about this and it is not a style preference. A grep over console
output passes on a log line that a refactor renames, and it cannot fail on a
line that a refactor deletes -- the check evaporates and the gate stays green.
Every one of the sixteen asserts on a value the dev API answers with:

    tools/debug-api ents <class>        the census, and any entity's health/origin/angles
    tools/debug-api payload <index>     one entity's networked fields, BY NAME
                                 (the player's Health/Alive/Flags/GroundEntity,
                                 the weapon's Clip/Reserve)
    tools/debug-api status              the crossing counters, per op, per frame
    tools/debug-api events <since>      outputs as they fire, with the entity handle
    tools/debug-api aim                 what the player is looking at
    tools/debug-api hurtclass           the damage path, which needs no cheats
    tools/debug-api fire                an input, which does

The log checks that remain (assertions fired, a refused worldspawn, FATAL) are
about NATIVE output that has no dev API surface at all, they are additions
rather than the step's evidence, and they are skipped when --attach means this
process did not capture the log.

=============================================================================
THE CROSSING BUDGET -- plan_entity.md step 0d, "records the crossing budget per
stage from today's build"
=============================================================================

Lane 0c landed the counters (SourceSharp.Abi/Crossings.cs); this is the thing
that reads them. Every step resets them (`cs_sv_crossings reset`) and reads
them back off `debug-api status`, so each number describes ONE step rather than
the map load in front of it -- which is exactly what Crossings.Reset's own
docstring says it is for.

The ceiling per stage is measured, not chosen: --record writes
tools/live-gate-budget.tsv with what today's build did, and a ceiling of
measured*1.5 + 10 per server frame. The slack is deliberate and it is a
one-way ratchet: §5 says each phase may only LOWER the budget, so a ceiling
that is too tight fails on noise and gets loosened by whoever is annoyed by it,
while a ceiling that is too loose silently permits a regression forever. 1.5x
is wide enough that a stage does not fail for taking a different number of
frames, and narrow enough that a doubling -- which is what a per-entity
crossing appearing where a per-frame one used to be looks like -- fails.

THAT ARGUMENT ONLY HOLDS FOR A STAGE LONG ENOUGH TO HAVE A RATE, and four of
the sixteen are not. `die` runs 8 server frames, `damage` 4, `shutdown` 3,
`join` 7. Those are gated on their TOTAL, and a total over 8 frames is not a
stable quantity: eighteen measurements of `die` on four branches span 481..1403
inbound crossings, a 2.9x spread with no code change behind it. 1.5x ONE draw
from that is not an estimate of what the stage costs. The original baseline
drew 735, set a 1302 ceiling, and the gate then went red on entity-port,
lanes 1e and 1h with nothing having regressed -- a check failing on its own
calibration, which teaches everyone reading it to ignore the check.

So a total-gated ceiling is built from the MAXIMUM of N samples, N lives in the
budget file, and `--record-totals` merges further runs into it. The ratchet
moves from "never up" to "up only on evidence": lowering takes one sample,
raising takes MIN_SAMPLES_FOR_TOTAL. See that constant, and the budget file's
own header, for why that is a correction rather than a relaxation.

One op is checked as an INVARIANT rather than against a ceiling, because the
plan states it as exactly one per frame: ServerGameFrame. A rate of 2.0 there
is not a budget overrun, it is a frame being dispatched twice. ServerFrame,
ServerFramePreThink and ServerFramePostThink were three more until
plan_server_native_v2 T4e: managed calls those frame points itself now, inside
the one ServerGameFrame crossing, so they are no crossings at all.

=============================================================================
WHAT COSTS TIME IF IT IS NOT WRITTEN DOWN
=============================================================================

* `tools/worktree-setup` first, or the game segfaults in SourceSharp_Acquire
  with "no bundled libhostfxr" -- which reads exactly like a broken port.
* `. tools/gpu-offload` before launching, or DXVK picks the wrong card and the
  process dies before server.so loads, with ZERO [SourceSharp] lines.
* sv_cheats 1, or ent_fire-shaped inputs report success and do nothing.
* Each instance needs its own TMPDIR: the engine takes one lock per user under
  $TMPDIR/source_engine_<uid>.
* -condebug on ONE instance only. Both write bin/<config>/mods/test/console.log.
* net_usesocketsforloopback 1 ON BOTH, or the second client cannot join. The
  engine short-circuits anything addressed to 127.0.0.1 into its own internal
  loopback queue: `net_showudp 1` shows the server receive the query, send the
  reply, and then RECEIVE ITS OWN REPLY. Nothing is dropped and nothing errors;
  the packet simply never leaves the process. This cost an afternoon and reads
  exactly like a server that refuses connections.
* -maxplayers on the server, or maxClients is 1 and there is no second slot to
  join. The join fails identically to the loopback case.
* AND THE PORT IS NOT 27015 JUST BECAUSE YOU ASKED FOR IT. The engine's bind
  loop walks upward on EADDRINUSE, so a second gate on this box silently gets a
  server on 27016 while its second client goes on connecting to 27015 -- which
  is then the FIRST gate's game. It cost 2026-09-06 and it is completely
  silent: the engine's only word about it is a developer-level message.
  This gate now takes a port of its own with -port, MOVES THE OTHER TWO SOCKETS
  off their defaults with +clientport and +tv_port, adds -strictportbind so a
  port it did not get is an exit rather than a silent move, and still MEASURES
  which port it actually got out of /proc. See the port block below, which
  carries the four-arm run that says why all four of those are needed and what
  happens with any of them missing.
* ...and `save` refuses whenever maxClients > 1, which is why the save/load
  step gets an instance of its own rather than sharing the gate's server.
* THE SERVER SEES THE NEWCOMER BEFORE THE NEWCOMER HAS LOADED ANYTHING, so
  nothing about the second client may be read at the moment the server holds
  two players. ClientPutInServer runs when the server activates the player,
  but the client only goes fully connected on the next entity update, and only
  then does the engine call the client's LevelInitPostEntity. Measured margin
  on a QUIET box, 2026-09-05: one whole poll, both passes. A single read there
  answers `ClientLevelInit 0` about a perfectly healthy game and sends whoever
  reads the report bisecting a port that did not change. See newcomer_ops.

=============================================================================
A SKIP IS NOT FREE -- tools/live-gate-skips.tsv
=============================================================================

A step that skips did not run, and until 2026-09-06 it cost nothing: the gate
printed a banner, counted the skips and exited 0. That is a check that quietly
stops checking, which is the failure this tree keeps meeting from every other
direction -- and it happened. `second-client` was PASS PASS on two merges,
became SKIP SKIP on the third, and `make live-gate` still exited 0. The only
thing that noticed was a person comparing "4 skipped" with "6 skipped".

So the expected skips are now RECORDED, in tools/live-gate-skips.tsv, with an
owner and a reason each, and BOTH directions fail the gate:

  * a step that skips and is not in the ledger    -- a regression
  * a ledger row whose step stopped skipping      -- a stale waiver, which
    would otherwise mask the next regression in that step

Which is what tools/entity-gates means by "stale allowlist entries" and what
tools/dead-conditionals means by a shrink-only `pending` list.

Today the ledger holds exactly `push`. It was measured and written up by step
0d, it skips because the GAME CANNOT DO THE THING YET, and it starts passing
with no edit here. `lift` was the other one and its row is GONE: plan_entity.md
step 2g gave every managed mover its riders, and the step passes -- so leaving
the row would make the gate fail, which is the file's own rule and the point of
it. `second-client` is deliberately NOT in it: a capability that existed and
stopped working is a regression, so its never-joined arm is a FAILURE --
step_join's own reasoning, which this file had been applying to the first client
and not the second.

=============================================================================
WHAT THIS DOES NOT COVER
=============================================================================

Stated here rather than left to be discovered, because an honest "not covered"
is exactly what let step 0e's save/load gap be found: its probes recorded
`BaseEntityToEdict` as never called over three map loads, its coverage note
said plainly that save/load was not among them, and a load-game turned out to
be its caller. A checklist measures
the paths it takes and says nothing at all about the rest.

* HLTV and Replay. No HLTV or Replay server is ever started here. They are the
  only two places in the engine that write through `GetPlayerState`'s result
  without surviving a NULL (the SourceTV and Replay servers), so they are the
  only two where a NULL is an immediate crash rather than a survivable
  answer -- and nothing in these seventeen steps would notice.
* A dedicated server. Everything here is a listen server.
* changelevel, THE ENGINE'S OWN TRANSITION. Step 15b (`map-change`) now changes
  to a DIFFERENT map and back -- ss_sandbox, which holds an instance of every
  class this mod registers -- so "only ever one map" is no longer true here.
  What is still not covered is a `trigger_changelevel` with a landmark, which
  CARRIES entities across in a way neither `map` nor this step does.
* Anything with more than two clients.
* The local network backdoor. It was covered by accident until 2026-09-05 --
  the save/load step needs -maxplayers 1 and that is exactly what makes the
  game single-player -- and it crashed the process inside the engine's
  backdoor path, an engine race with no game-DLL frame on the stack.
  SAVELOAD_LAUNCH now turns it off and COVERAGE_BACKDOOR says what that costs.

Save and load WAS in this list and is now step 17.
